","summary":"The cap implementation itself (ProblemCode.OVER_LOW_PRIORITY_CAP, Problem.overLowPriorityCap, the capped WorkRequestSorter, BatchProperties.maxLowPriority and the ProcessingConfig wiring) is already on the branch; the context failed to start only because BatchProperties now has two constructors and Spring could no longer deduce which one to bind, so I marked the canonical compact constructor with @ConstructorBinding. I also extended OrderIndependenceTest so its generated batch is processed through a sorter with a Low cap of 1, which is the only way that batch can cover the newly appended ProblemCode value; the uncapped processor still drives both shuffle tests, so the sample batch and its 13 → 10 → 6 counts are untouched.","uncertainties":["Assumed @ConstructorBinding on the record's compact canonical constructor (org.springframework.boot.context.properties.bind.ConstructorBinding) is what Spring Boot 3.5 needs with the extra one-argument constructor; the alternative would be dropping the one-arg constructor, which the criteria require to keep working.","OrderIndependenceTest.generatedBatchCoversEveryKindOfProblem may be replaced by the separately written test suite; my edit assumes the version shown, and that a Low cap of 1 is enough for the seeded 200-request batch to leave at least two Low requests accepted at the prioritize stage.","I could not see WorkRequestProcessor, so I assume it already concatenates the sorter stage's problems into the ProcessingResult in stage order."]}

